    "content": "The Federal Government of Somalia has announced that it will remain neutral in the escalating tensions between Ethiopia and Eritrea. In an interview with the BBC, Deputy Minister of Foreign Affairs Hassan Mohamed Ali Nadara stated that Somalia is closely monitoring the regional situation but will not align with either side. He emphasized that Mogadishu's position is to preserve peace and pursue a diplomatic resolution to the ongoing dispute.\n\nThe deputy minister's remarks come amid growing friction between Ethiopia and Eritrea, though the specific triggers of the current escalation have not been detailed. Somalia has previously called for calm and dialogue in the Horn of Africa, a region with a history of interconnected conflicts. The government's stance reflects its broader foreign policy priority of maintaining stability while avoiding entanglement in neighboring disputes.\n\nThis development is particularly significant for the Somali diaspora, which maintains strong family, business, and social ties across the region. Many diaspora members have relatives in Ethiopia and Eritrea, and any heightened conflict could disrupt travel, trade, and remittance flows. Additionally, the Somali government's neutrality may be seen as an effort to protect its own security interests, given the presence of Somali refugees in both countries and the potential for spillover effects.\n\nSomalia's decision to stay out of the Ethiopia-Eritrea dispute also highlights its focus on domestic challenges, including the fight against al-Shabab and ongoing state-building efforts. By avoiding regional entanglements, Mogadishu aims to preserve its diplomatic relationships and concentrate on internal stabilization. The diaspora has often played a role in supporting peace initiatives back home, and many will be watching closely to see how this neutrality affects Somalia's standing in the region.\n\nWhile the deputy minister did not provide further details on the nature of the tensions, his comments suggest that Somalia will continue to advocate for dialogue. The international community, including the African Union and the United Nations, has frequently urged restraint in the Horn of Africa. For the diaspora, the hope is that diplomatic efforts will prevent any wider conflict that could impact families and communities across borders.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/13/soomaaliya-oo-dhexdhexaad-ka-noqotay-xiisadda-itoobiya-iyo-eritrea/",
